.TH LEXCONV 1 "October 2026" "nylex 0.2"
.SH NAME
lexconv \- nylex documents to and from RTF, Word, OpenDocument, Markdown
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lexconv
[
.B \-n
|
.B \-a
] [
.B \-w
.I width
] [
.B \-j
]
.I from to
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Lexconv
turns a document of
.IR nylex (1)
(and of LEX) into a file for another word processor, or such a file into
a document.
The formats go by the extensions of the files:
.TP
.B .rtf
Rich Text Format (Word, LibreOffice, WordPad, TextEdit ...); written
and read
.TP
.B .docx
Word (Office Open XML); written and read
.TP
.B .odt
OpenDocument text (LibreOffice); written and read
.TP
.BR .md ", " .markdown
Markdown; written and read
.TP
.BR .html ", " .htm
a web page; written only
.TP
.BR .txt ", " .text
plain text in UTF-8; written and read
.PP
A file of any other extension is a LEX document (\c
.BR .brv ,
.BR .ltr ...).
Two of the other formats may be given as well: the text goes from one to
the other.
In
.IR nylex ,
X in the document menu runs
.I lexconv
(the record
.BR *=CONV ,
the menu switch option /H): it asks for the document from and the one
to, as
.B BREV
and
.BR BREV.DOCX ,
or
.B RAPPORT.MD
and
.BR RAPPORT .
.SS Out of a LEX document
A LEX document is lines laid out on rulers; out of it come the
paragraphs again.
Lines that were broken by word wrap (the next word did not fit before M)
or justified (on a J ruler, out to M) are joined into a paragraph, which
is justified when its ruler was J.
A line centred or put against M stays so.
A line of columns (pieces of text two or more spaces apart: a table,
tab stops) gets tab stops at its columns, numbers ending at theirs (in
Markdown, HTML and text it is kept as it is, in a fixed font).
.B .T
and
.B .F
become the page header and footer, with a page number field for
.B (SIDA:
or
.BR (PAGE: ;
.B .P
a page break,
.B .N
a footnote (at its place); the characters of
.B .C
for a pound sign, a space, a hard hyphen and a footnote's mark are
turned into those, and the other print time lines are left out.
Attributes 1 and 4 are underlining, 2 and 5 bold, 3 italic.
Markdown gets
.BR ** bold ** ,
.BR * italic *
and
.BR <u> underlined </u> ,
a line of columns a block of code, and a centred bold line a heading.
.SS Into a LEX document
The paragraphs are laid out on an F ruler of the width given (the M at
column 69, as in the document types of
.IR nylex 's
system files), a J ruler for justified ones, with an empty line between
paragraphs (unless the file has empty paragraphs of its own); centred
and right-aligned ones are put so.
Headings are bold, the first level centred.
Lists, with their marks, and quotations are indented; tables and text at
tab stops become lines of columns; a page break is
.BR .P ,
the header and footer
.B .T
and
.BR .F .
Underlining, bold and italic become attributes 1, 2 and 3.
Pictures, footnotes, comments and fields other than the page number are
left out; links keep their text and, after it, their address.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-n
the LEX document is in 7-bit Swedish ([ \e ] { | } the letters
A-umlaut, O-umlaut, A-ring and their lower case), as
.IR nylex 's
Swedish system file keeps it.
.TP
.B \-a
the LEX document is ASCII and Latin-1 (the English system file).
Without either,
.B $LEXNAT
decides (1 or 0;
.I nylex
sets it), else
.BR \-n .
.TP
.BI \-w " width"
the column of M for documents made (69).
.TP
.B \-j
paragraphs justified (a J ruler) whatever the file says.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
LEXNAT as above.
.SH SEE ALSO
nylex(1), lexspell(1).
.SH NOTES
Word and OpenDocument files are zip files: they are written stored (not
compressed), which every program reads, and read with an inflate of
.IR lexconv 's
own; nothing else is needed.
The older Word format
.RB ( .doc )
is not read or written: Word and LibreOffice save
.B .docx
and
.BR .rtf .
